94139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725566 1064 "**MTH 256 — Recitation 5 Activity**\n\nDue: 2026-10-28\nDate imported: 2026-10-27\nSource: [256-R5.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FR-5%2F256-R5.pdf)\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDeadline source: [Canvas Recitation 5 Activity](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/courses/2064169/assignments/10722770), checked 2026-10-27; Wednesday by 11:59 p.m.\n\nStudy answers and explanations are independently authored additions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nThis active-learning activity teaches the process for Problems 2–5 on WebWork 3. Work through each requested intermediate model and calculation. Use hours in Problem 1, years since 1989 in Problem 2, and minutes in Problems 3–4. Exact values are provided as study keys; decimal approximations in explanations are optional, since the PDF specifies no rounding rule. Pages 3, 5, and 7 contain only workspace, not additional problems." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725566 1066 #uuid "2aaf9fe9-0dc2-502c-9e7b-c55af43b827a" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725566 1067 "Assignment information" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725567 1058 #edb/ref 17592186725734 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725567 1062 #uuid "2bcecac5-0847-59f5-83d9-b8b88810ad74" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725567 1063 #edb/ref 17592186725591 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725568 1058 #edb/ref 17592186725599 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725568 1062 #uuid "2c6eac49-4dd3-58d2-a12f-78e55aa89ddc" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725568 1063 #edb/ref 17592186725741 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725569 1075 #edb/ref 17592186725721 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725569 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725569 1077 "roots" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725569 1078 #edb/ref 17592186725721 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725569 1079 #uuid "2d46db2f-3c33-51a8-84d4-a47e732547ad" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725570 1084 #edb/ref 17592186725565 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725570 1084 #edb/ref 17592186725598 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725570 1084 #edb/ref 17592186725613 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725570 1085 "Source question: [WeBWorK Problem 5](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_3/5).\n\nA tank contains 100 kg of salt in 1,000 L of water. A salt solution at 0.05 kg/L enters at 6 L/min. The mixture is well mixed and drains at the same rate." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725570 1086 #uuid "2e4a8ff4-b5a4-58ff-aa56-54620f973f94" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725570 1087 #edb/ref 17592186725565 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725561 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725571 1071 "**(c)** Substitute the value from (b) into the expression for temperature from (a). $T(t)=$ {{temperature}} degrees Celsius.\n\nNotation: $e^z=\\exp(z)$ denotes the natural exponential." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725571 1072 #uuid "2fa40454-60b7-5fb3-951d-a5b75a830926" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725571 1074 "$e^{-kt}=e^{(t/10)\\ln(12/17)}=(12/17)^{t/10}$. This yields 20 at $t=0$ and 15 at $t=10$, and tends to the cooler temperature 3." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725572 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725555 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725572 1071 "**(c)** How long will it take for the population to reach 2,230? Time $=$ {{time}} hours." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725572 1072 #uuid "31d571e3-98f5-5b76-83b0-4a3a8b4cdf95" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725572 1074 "$340\\cdot4^{t/4}=2230$. Taking logarithms gives $t=4\\ln(2230/340)/\\ln4\\approx5.4269$ hours." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725573 1058 #edb/ref 17592186725649 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725573 1062 #uuid "3224a30b-1894-5809-83cf-3b1ef4abaf15" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725573 1063 #edb/ref 17592186725682 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725574 1080 "\\frac18" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725574 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725574 1083 #uuid "34dcda8d-8b1f-5ffc-91ae-f4c24826be6d" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725575 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725692 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725575 1071 "**(c)** Draw the trajectory in the $x$–$v$ plane corresponding to $x(0)=-1.5$, $v(0)=1$.\n\n![Source spring phase plane](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FWHW-3%2Fspring-phase-plane.png)" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725575 1072 #uuid "3798d1bc-675f-53ab-bf65-04be31148457" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725575 1074 "Use the level curve through the given point, with $x$ horizontal and $v$ vertical. Above the horizontal axis, $x\\prime=v>0$, so motion goes right; at the rightmost point $v\\prime=-(k/m)x<0$, so it goes downward. Do not assume numerical values for $m$ and $k$ from an unlabeled family of curves." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725657 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725576 1071 "**(b)** The question states that, in 1988, three teams found that the Shroud of Turin contained about 91% of the carbon-14 in freshly made cloth of the same material (source attribution: The New York Times, October 18, 1988). According to these data, how old was it in 1988?\n\nAge $=$ {{age}} years." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725576 1072 #uuid "3822e6fa-8979-51db-b3a6-0d28acf60b95" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725576 1074 "The source data imply $C(t)/C(0)=0.91=e^{-kt}$. Thus $t=-\\ln(0.91)/k=5597\\ln(100/91)/\\ln2\\approx761.54$ years. This solves the supplied mathematical scenario; it is the age in 1988, not a calendar date." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725577 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048870 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725577 1089 #edb/ref 17592186725601 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725577 1090 #uuid "3856bf8c-ae02-56de-807f-d88c22c5d86b" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725578 1075 #edb/ref 17592186725714 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725578 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725578 1077 "work" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725578 1078 #edb/ref 17592186725714 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725578 1079 #uuid "38d573d7-7c6c-548e-8209-8381bc809189" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725579 1058 #edb/ref 17592186725718 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725579 1062 #uuid "39fed138-2442-50ec-ae7e-5e53529e449b" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725579 1063 #edb/ref 17592186725608 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725580 1075 #edb/ref 17592186725595 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725580 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725580 1077 "time" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725580 1078 #edb/ref 17592186725595 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725580 1079 #uuid "3a5fed1c-174d-5a92-a53d-961b89f8ec65" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725581 1058 #edb/ref 17592186725567 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725581 1062 #uuid "3a63bc66-eeae-5372-ad11-2f44314e2732" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725581 1063 #edb/ref 17592186725653 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725582 1062 #uuid "3a752911-1b77-586a-93ca-0978e7197a19" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725582 1063 #edb/ref 17592186725629 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725583 1080 "\\frac{\\ln2}{5597}" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725583 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725583 1083 #uuid "3b79c322-6598-546f-ad9c-ecda3e0b8cee" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725584 1080 "2000e^{rt}" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725584 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725584 1083 #uuid "3bc5c922-566d-5d7c-8841-0769cd3ade9e" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725585 1080 "189e^{-kt}" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725585 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725585 1083 #uuid "3c5239a2-4e80-5e01-a36e-eee67d03b448" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725647 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725586 1071 "**(d)** Solve $Q(t)=50$ to find the years after 1989 until 50 kg remains. $t=$ {{time}} years." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725586 1072 #uuid "3cb874d3-a29e-5091-a052-256934b25d94" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725586 1074 "$e^{-kt}=50/189$ gives $t=\\ln(189/50)/k$. Hence $t=24110\\ln(189/50)/\\ln2\\approx46252.3$ years after 1989. This is an elapsed time, not a calendar year." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725552 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725719 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725587 1071 "**(d)** Solve the energy equation in (b) for $v$ in terms of $\\theta$, then replace $v$ with $d\\theta/dt$ to obtain a first-order ODE with constants $g,E$. Give both branches.\n\n$v=$ {{v}}; $\\dfrac{d\\theta}{dt}=$ {{rhs}}." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725587 1072 #uuid "3cf27de0-dc25-5831-809d-94a6bd49b113" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725587 1074 "Multiply the energy equation by 2 and isolate $v^2$. The radicand must be nonnegative. Rocking trajectories change sign at turning points; rotations with $E>2g$ retain one sign. The equilibrium states are also included when the radicand vanishes at a constant angle." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725588 1080 "\\frac{5597\\ln(100/91)}{\\ln2}" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725588 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725588 1083 #uuid "3ddac532-4811-5b32-ad98-a1a19281d322" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725589 1075 #edb/ref 17592186725694 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725589 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725589 1077 "concentration" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725589 1078 #edb/ref 17592186725694 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725589 1079 #uuid "3e86b2d5-29e6-5939-a05d-08cd2678b5a0" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725590 1080 "\\frac{24110\\ln(189/50)}{\\ln2}" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725590 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725590 1083 #uuid "3f1007c1-6e26-5296-a39f-bee1a9d8c2c3" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725591 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725675 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725591 1071 "**(b)** Find the implicit solutions of (a) and derive $\\frac12v^2+g(1-\\cos\\theta)=E$, with $E$ constant." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725591 1072 #uuid "401603bc-170b-538f-ad71-ed8999d6b10b" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725591 1074 "The antiderivative of $-g\\sin\\theta$ is $g\\cos\\theta$, not $-g\\cos\\theta$. Check conservation directly: $d[v^2/2+g(1-\\cos\\theta)]/dt=v(v\\prime+g\\sin\\theta)=0$." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725592 1084 #edb/ref 17592186725559 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725592 1084 #edb/ref 17592186725669 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725592 1084 #edb/ref 17592186725732 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725592 1085 "Source question: [WeBWorK Problem 2](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_3/2).\n\nA bacteria culture starts with 340 bacteria and grows at a rate proportional to its size. After 4 hours there are 1,360 bacteria." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725592 1086 #uuid "402d7e8e-8b30-53fb-af25-20e73b0b1230" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725592 1087 #edb/ref 17592186725669 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725593 1075 #edb/ref 17592186725622 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725593 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725593 1077 "work" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725593 1078 #edb/ref 17592186725622 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725593 1079 #uuid "41660829-f5f6-55d2-b5a1-8ee30fe7859d" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725648 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725594 1071 "**(b)** The functions $y_1=e^x$ and $y_2=xe^x$ solve $y\\!\\prime\\!\\prime-2y\\prime+y=0$. Use the Wronskian to show that they form a fundamental set of solutions. Include a statement explaining your reasoning for full credit." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725594 1072 #uuid "42b35269-3dbb-518e-be5a-da6547caaed1" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725594 1074 "The product rule in $(xe^x)\\prime=(1+x)e^x$ is essential. Cancellation leaves $e^{2x}$, not zero. Because the equation is second-order and homogeneous, the independent pair spans its solution space." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725595 1080 "\\frac{10\\ln50}{\\ln10}" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725595 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725595 1083 #uuid "43348720-23d7-5a2f-a2f6-b3710f7b9375" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725596 1080 "1360\\sqrt2" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725596 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725596 1083 #uuid "452408df-0d58-5249-86b8-071bfce53497" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725597 1080 "30-15e^{-t/15}" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725597 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725597 1083 #uuid "454b4315-8409-508c-b13d-b8878b042cc8" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725598 1062 #uuid "45ada711-e5f0-5d0c-91c5-8acc41b3a7cc" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725598 1063 #edb/ref 17592186725551 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725599 1058 #edb/ref 17592186725542 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725599 1062 #uuid "47468602-d37e-54b2-93bb-07d4fa1f89d4" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725599 1063 #edb/ref 17592186725736 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725600 1084 #edb/ref 17592186725603 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725600 1084 #edb/ref 17592186725671 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725600 1084 #edb/ref 17592186725704 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725600 1084 #edb/ref 17592186725737 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725600 1085 "Source question: assignment PDF page 2, parts (a)–(d).\n\nFrom 1944 through 1989 Hanford produced 67.4 metric tons of plutonium. The worksheet estimates that 189 kg of plutonium-239 waste was disposed of there; its stated half-life is 24,110 years. Determine how many years after 1989 must pass until 50 kg remains, through the steps below. The worksheet cites [PNNL-21651](https://www.pnnl.gov/main/publications/external/technical_reports/PNNL-21651.pdf) for its historical context. Use the given 189 kg as the initial radioactive-waste mass, not the production total." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725600 1086 #uuid "47487696-3bc2-5709-ae88-e6a9369af351" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725600 1087 #edb/ref 17592186725704 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725601 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725606 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725601 1071 "Source question: [WeBWorK Problem 1](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_3/1).\n\nA 50 kg skydiver jumps from an airplane. Find her terminal velocity in miles per hour, assuming the free-fall linear-drag constant is $k=10\\,\\mathrm{kg/s}$ (no parachute). The answer must be negative for downward velocity.\n\nTerminal velocity $=$ {{velocity}} mph." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725601 1072 #uuid "486e23c7-d519-5894-9f25-30026dac21d3" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725601 1074 "With upward positive, $mv\\prime=-mg-kv$. At terminal velocity $0=-mg-kv_T$, so $v_T=-mg/k=-49$ m/s using the standard course value $g=9.8$ m/s². Convert using 1 mile = 1609.344 m: $v_T=-49(3600/1609.344)\\approx-109.6106$ mph. The source does not restate $g$; the study answer explicitly uses $9.8$." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725602 1080 "$W(y_1,y_2)=\\begin{vmatrix}e^{2x}&e^{-x}\\\\2e^{2x}&-e^{-x}\\end{vmatrix}=-3e^x\\ne0$ for every $x\\in\\mathbb R$. The two given functions are solutions of the homogeneous second-order linear equation, whose coefficients are continuous on $\\mathbb R$. The nonzero Wronskian proves linear independence, so the pair is a fundamental set on $\\mathbb R$." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725602 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725602 1083 #uuid "499b012f-3fbc-50dd-8e0e-ed5f6307ae72" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725603 1058 #edb/ref 17592186725671 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725603 1062 #uuid "4c4d872b-231a-5930-aeb2-54372caa22ca" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725603 1063 #edb/ref 17592186725617 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725604 1058 #edb/ref 17592186725701 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725604 1062 #uuid "4d3b8e24-9e62-54d7-b544-c54337a94b54" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725604 1063 #edb/ref 17592186725624 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725605 1043 #edb/ref 17592186725619 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725605 1043 #edb/ref 17592186725628 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725605 1043 #edb/ref 17592186725635 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725605 1043 #edb/ref 17592186725642 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725605 1043 #edb/ref 17592186725656 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725605 1043 #edb/ref 17592186725703 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725605 1043 #edb/ref 17592186725713 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725605 1043 #edb/ref 17592186725715 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725605 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045461 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725605 1045 "MTH 256 — OHW-3" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725605 1046 #edb/ref 17592186725715 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725605 1047 #inst "2026-10-31T06:59:59.000Z" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725605 1048 #edb/ref 17592186405205 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725605 1053 #uuid "50735fa7-f2bc-59f6-87a2-41e1d7c393c9" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186725549 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725606 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725606 1077 "velocity" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725606 1078 #edb/ref 17592186725549 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725606 1079 #uuid "52f908e1-b5a9-5c10-867f-70233043aa60" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725607 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049053 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725607 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049131 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725607 1089 #edb/ref 17592186725720 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725607 1090 #uuid "53522c67-2767-5c22-a2f1-acd55bfd4181" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725608 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725738 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725608 1071 "**(c)** Determine the population after 5 hours. $P(5)=$ {{population}} bacteria." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725608 1072 #uuid "54539418-360d-52d8-a017-eeeff799e9be" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725608 1074 "$P(5)=2000e^{(\\ln10)/2}=2000\\sqrt{10}\\approx6324.56$. The model predicts about 6,325 bacteria if a whole-number estimate is desired." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725609 1075 #edb/ref 17592186725678 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725609 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725609 1077 "v" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725609 1078 #edb/ref 17592186725678 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725609 1079 #uuid "546ab586-8a8c-5712-9c93-5ea1a34ab84e" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725610 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725645 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725610 1071 "**(a)** Solve the IVP for $P(t)$ in terms of time $t$ in hours and the constant $r$. $P(t)=$ {{population}}.\n\nNotation: $e^z=\\exp(z)$ denotes the natural exponential." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725610 1072 #uuid "566e4a0c-0d60-5aa9-8735-e2b797e78a03" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725610 1074 "Separate $dP/P=r\\,dt$ to obtain $P=Ce^{rt}$. Applying $P(0)=2000$ gives $C=2000$. Differentiation verifies $P\\prime=rP$." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725611 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048127 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725611 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049053 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725611 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049854 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725611 1089 #edb/ref 17592186725592 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725611 1090 #uuid "59ae15f8-3267-5783-b446-a0d417587a9f" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725612 1064 "**MTH 256 — Written Homework 3**\n\nDue: 2026-10-30\nDate imported: 2026-10-27\nSource: [256-WHW3.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FWHW-3%2F256-WHW3.pdf)\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDeadline source: [Canvas Written Homework 3](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/courses/2064169/assignments/10722772), checked 2026-10-27; Friday by 11:59 p.m., also confirmed by the user.\n\nStudy answers and explanations are independently authored additions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nRetain all derivations and drawings. Problem 3 explicitly requires a statement explaining why each pair is a fundamental set of solutions. The physical spring interpretation uses $m>0$ and $k>0$; the source gives no numerical values for them. In Problem 2 the PDF prints the acceleration unit as “$m/x$”; this is a source typo. Use the supplied equation with the stated numerical value $g=9.8$ (for a one-meter pendulum, the coefficient is acceleration divided by length)." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725612 1066 #uuid "5a9860e7-1a8a-5141-b0f2-7104a8afa2cd" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725612 1067 "Assignment information" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725613 1058 #edb/ref 17592186725598 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725613 1062 #uuid "5c1cafe8-f21b-5c04-8b52-fd4a782919a7" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725613 1063 #edb/ref 17592186725680 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725661 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725667 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725614 1071 "**(a)** Substitute $T_m=3$ and $T_0=20$ into the cooling IVP, then solve for $T(t)$ in terms of $k$ and time $t$ in minutes.\n\n$T\\prime+kT=$ {{forcing}}, $T(0)=20$; $T(t)=$ {{temperature}}.\n\nNotation: $e^z=\\exp(z)$ denotes the natural exponential." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725614 1072 #uuid "5cff68ac-53de-5096-b9b0-f63599fcec11" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725614 1074 "The IVP is $T\\prime+kT=3k$, $T(0)=20$. Subtract the ambient temperature: $U=T-3$ satisfies $U\\prime=-kU$, $U(0)=17$. Thus $T=3+17e^{-kt}$; substitution checks both the ODE and initial value." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725615 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049053 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725615 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050451 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725615 1089 #edb/ref 17592186725742 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725615 1090 #uuid "5e1b111a-8bf5-5df7-9ed2-ba6779145fb2" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725616 1058 #edb/ref 17592186725670 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725616 1062 #uuid "5f13db05-7d05-5268-acf9-795b210da28d" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725616 1063 #edb/ref 17592186725548 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725683 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725617 1071 "**(c)** Substitute the value of $k$ into $Q(t)$ and determine the mass after 10,000 years. $Q(10000)=$ {{mass}} kg.\n\nNotation: $e^z=\\exp(z)$ denotes the natural exponential." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725617 1072 #uuid "61054895-b229-5690-97a0-37fe777c79c8" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725617 1074 "$Q(10000)=189e^{-10000\\ln2/24110}=189\\cdot2^{-10000/24110}\\approx141.78$ kg. Less than one half-life has passed, so the mass must exceed $94.5$ kg." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725662 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725618 1071 "**(a)** Derive a separable differential equation involving $x$, $v$, and $dv/dx$. Show the change of variable." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725618 1072 #uuid "62476e8a-0f97-598e-b923-47a29016fc8b" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725618 1074 "Do not replace $x\\!\\prime\\!\\prime$ by $dv/dx$ alone: the chain rule contributes $dx/dt=v$. At turning points the position parametrization can fail; the energy identity obtained in (b) extends through them." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725619 1058 #edb/ref 17592186725703 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725619 1062 #uuid "646619cf-f890-5868-8e5f-4ecb5ef7fe8a" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725619 1063 #edb/ref 17592186725621 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725620 1075 #edb/ref 17592186725540 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725620 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725620 1077 "rhs" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725620 1078 #edb/ref 17592186725540 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725620 1079 #uuid "64a778fb-3467-55a0-92a8-a62e339adb7c" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725621 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049774 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725621 1089 #edb/ref 17592186725570 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725621 1090 #uuid "66c4ae3f-79b8-52d8-9ead-be71a8ddfff0" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725622 1080 "An example rocking trajectory has $(\\theta(0),v(0))=(0,\\sqrt{2g})$, so $E=g<2g$ and the closed curve is $v^2=2g\\cos\\theta$, $-\\pi/2\\leq\\theta\\leq\\pi/2$. Draw its upper branch rightward and lower branch leftward. For rotation choose $E=3g>2g$. The positive branch $v=\\sqrt{2g(2+\\cos\\theta)}$ through $(0,\\sqrt{6g})$ moves right; the negative branch through $(0,-\\sqrt{6g})$ moves left. With increasing angle defined as counterclockwise, these represent counterclockwise and clockwise physical rotation respectively.\n\n![Authored reference: three pendulum trajectories](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FWHW-3%2Fauthored-pendulum-trajectories.png)" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725622 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725622 1083 #uuid "67a5d355-5c7c-57fd-910e-da30d96c8fd6" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725623 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725545 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725623 1071 "**(a)** Express the population after $t$ hours as a function of $t$. Population $=$ {{population}}.\n\nNotation: $e^z=\\exp(z)$ denotes the natural exponential." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725623 1072 #uuid "6964efcd-98a8-5b89-93a0-5b9676ae8c53" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725623 1074 "$P=P_0e^{rt}$ and $P_0=340$. Since $1360/340=4$, $e^{4r}=4$, so $P(t)=340e^{t\\ln4/4}=340\\cdot4^{t/4}$." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725624 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725578 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725624 1071 "**(b)** Find the implicit solutions to the separable equation in (a) and derive the conservation of energy equation $\\frac m2v^2+\\frac k2x^2=E$, where $E$ is a constant." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725624 1072 #uuid "69ec9f25-4f97-5af0-94a2-64e81e082888" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725624 1074 "The factors $1/2$ come from integrating $v$ and $x$. Independently, $d(mv^2/2+kx^2/2)/dt=v(mv\\prime+kx)=0$ along Newton’s equation, including at $v=0$." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725705 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725625 1071 "**(a)** The functions $y_1=e^{2x}$ and $y_2=e^{-x}$ solve $y\\!\\prime\\!\\prime-y\\prime-2y=0$. Use the Wronskian to show that they form a fundamental set of solutions. Include a statement explaining your reasoning for full credit." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725625 1072 #uuid "6a5d6dc4-a997-5c41-ad7f-595dd4c4af37" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725625 1074 "Use functions in the first row and their derivatives in the second. A determinant alone does not finish the requested explanation: state nonvanishing, linear independence, and why two independent solutions form a fundamental set for this second-order equation." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725626 1075 #edb/ref 17592186725673 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725626 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725626 1077 "rate" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725626 1078 #edb/ref 17592186725673 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725626 1079 #uuid "6acc04b3-66c2-5317-8613-972c3bb0a5cc" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725627 1075 #edb/ref 17592186725716 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725627 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725627 1077 "time" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725627 1078 #edb/ref 17592186725716 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725627 1079 #uuid "6bca0ef5-bdb7-59c1-b2df-22172c9272b8" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725628 1058 #edb/ref 17592186725713 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725628 1062 #uuid "710697c5-874c-5d03-9b8e-e037987b2eb8" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725628 1063 #edb/ref 17592186725611 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725644 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725629 1071 "**(d)** Determine the sugar mass after 30 minutes. $Q(30)=$ {{mass}} pounds.\n\nNotation: $e^z=\\exp(z)$ denotes the natural exponential." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725629 1072 #uuid "710a65ec-c084-5806-a566-9fa6eebba34e" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725629 1074 "Substitute $t=30$: $Q(30)=30-15e^{-2}\\approx27.9700$ pounds. This lies between the initial 15 pounds and the limiting 30 pounds." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725630 1075 #edb/ref 17592186725541 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725630 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725630 1077 "rate" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725630 1078 #edb/ref 17592186725541 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725630 1079 #uuid "714d099b-215c-5adc-a485-6ac23688a757" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725631 1080 "-1+23(13/23)^5" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725631 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725631 1083 #uuid "7158de45-0da7-56b2-9e27-609a073b284b" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725632 1062 #uuid "741ede89-eee5-5be7-ad4f-85db12bd0e8d" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725632 1063 #edb/ref 17592186725684 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725633 1058 #edb/ref 17592186725686 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725633 1062 #uuid "7579277a-1be0-52da-8848-e52c9ee879d0" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725633 1063 #edb/ref 17592186725610 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725634 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725735 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725634 1071 "**(a)** The rate of change of radioactive mass is proportional to the remaining mass: $Q\\prime(t)=-kQ(t)$, $Q(0)=189$. Solve the IVP, leaving $k$ undetermined. $Q(t)=$ {{mass}} kg.\n\nNotation: $e^z=\\exp(z)$ denotes the natural exponential." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725634 1072 #uuid "760f3d2a-b4f9-5faa-9809-2255158fd7ae" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725634 1074 "Separate and integrate to get $Q=Ce^{-kt}$. The initial condition gives $C=189$. Its derivative is $-kQ$ and its initial value is 189." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725635 1058 #edb/ref 17592186725628 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725635 1062 #uuid "762fd679-7500-5fc4-9852-0e9b05be8a57" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725635 1063 #edb/ref 17592186725577 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725636 1080 "189\\exp(-10000\\ln2/24110)" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725636 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725636 1083 #uuid "76f4f2a9-a51c-5a26-a655-15374984d328" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725626 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725637 1071 "**(b)** Determine $r$ from $P(10)=20000$. $r=$ {{rate}} per hour." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725637 1072 #uuid "77091b57-11a1-5e2b-a033-95b1455f530e" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725637 1074 "$2000e^{10r}=20000$ implies $e^{10r}=10$, so $r=\\ln(10)/10\\approx0.2302585$ per hour." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725638 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725664 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725638 1068 #edb/ref 17592186725707 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725638 1071 "**(b)** Let $Q(t)$ be the sugar mass in pounds. The model is $Q\\prime=$ [rate sugar enters] $-$ [rate sugar leaves], $Q(0)=15$. Sugar enters at $(1/4)(8)=2$ lb/min and leaves at $8C(t)$ lb/min. Express the concentration using $Q(t)$ and the constant volume, then write the ODE.\n\n$C(t)=$ {{concentration}}; $Q\\prime(t)=$ {{rhs}}, $Q(0)=15$. In these input expressions, use $Q$ for $Q(t)$." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725638 1072 #uuid "77550cc4-c025-57c1-91d3-15afeed43b57" 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725638 1074 "Equal inflow and outflow keep volume at 120 gallons. Thus $C=Q/120$, the outflow mass rate is $8Q/120=Q/15$, and $Q\\prime=2-Q/15$. Both terms have units lb/min." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725639 1084 #edb/ref 17592186725553 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725639 1084 #edb/ref 17592186725582 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725639 1084 #edb/ref 17592186725616 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725639 1084 #edb/ref 17592186725670 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [17592186725639 1085 "Source question: assignment PDF page 6, parts (a)–(d); page 7 is workspace.\n\nA tank initially contains 15 pounds of sugar in 120 gallons of water. A solution containing $1/4$ pound of sugar per gallon enters at 8 gal/min, and the well-mixed solution leaves at the same rate." 13194139542354 17592186502323 true] [1759218672